## INPUT PATH SECURITY

### Path Validation (FolderScanService.cs)

Implement `ValidateFolderPath(string requestedPath)` as the FIRST call in any method
that accepts a user-supplied folder path.

```csharp
public void ValidateFolderPath(string requestedPath)
{
    // Step 1: Resolve to absolute, canonical path (resolves ../ traversal, symlinks)
    string canonical;
    try
    {
        canonical = Path.GetFullPath(requestedPath);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new SecurityException($"Invalid folder path: {ex.Message}");
    }

    // Step 2: Check against allowlist
    var allowedPaths = _options.AllowedBasePaths
        .Select(p => Path.GetFullPath(p))
        .ToList();

    bool isAllowed = allowedPaths.Any(allowed =>
        canonical.StartsWith(allowed, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

    if (!isAllowed)
    {
        // IMPORTANT: Do NOT include the requested path in the exception message
        // that reaches the HTTP response — log it internally only.
        _logger.LogWarning("Path traversal attempt rejected. Requested: {Path}", canonical);
        throw new SecurityException(
            "The requested folder path is not in the list of allowed extraction paths. " +
            "Contact your administrator to add this path to AllowedBasePaths in appsettings.json.");
    }

    // Step 3: Check directory exists
    if (!Directory.Exists(canonical))
        throw new DirectoryNotFoundException(
            "The specified folder does not exist or is not accessible.");
}
```

The sanitized exception message (without the path) is what gets returned to the HTTP client.
The full path is logged only to the server-side log file.

