## REVISION CONFLICT DETECTION

### Unique Constraint and Conflict Strategy

Add a unique index in EF Core on the combination `(PartNumber, Operation, Revision)`:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<ToolingRecord>(e => {
    // ...existing indexes...
    e.HasIndex(x => new { x.PartNumber, x.Operation, x.Revision })
     .HasDatabaseName("IX_ToolingRecords_PartOpRev");
     // NOTE: This is NOT a unique constraint — multiple tools exist per part/op/rev.
     // The uniqueness constraint belongs at the job level, enforced in the pipeline.
});
```

In `ExtractionPipelineService`, after parsing, before inserting:
```
1. Query: SELECT DISTINCT PartNumber, Operation, Revision FROM ToolingRecords
          WHERE PartNumber = @pn AND Operation = @op
          AND Revision != @rev
   If rows exist → a different revision of this part/operation already exists.

2. Log.Warning("REVISION CONFLICT: {PartNumber} {Operation} already has revision(s)
   [{existingRevs}] in the database. Inserting new revision {newRev}. Manual review
   recommended to confirm which revision is authoritative.");

3. Insert the new records anyway (latest revision does NOT automatically delete old ones).

4. Set a flag on all new records: RevisionConflictDetected = true (add bool field to ToolingRecord).

5. Write a conflict notice to ./failed_extractions/revision_conflicts.log:
   Timestamp | SourceFile | PartNumber | Operation | ExistingRevisions | NewRevision
```

Add `RevisionConflictDetected` bool field to `ToolingRecord`. Add to Excel export
"Amended Files" sheet — rename it to "Review Required" and include both amended records
AND revision-conflict records. Color revision-conflict rows in light red (`#F8D7DA`).

