## PRODUCTION HARDENING — KNOWN ISSUES & MANDATORY SOLUTIONS

The following issues have been identified through production analysis. All solutions in this
section are **mandatory** — implement each one exactly as specified. These are not optional
improvements; they represent known failure modes in aerospace-grade tooling extraction.

---

### ISSUE 1 — PDF RENDERING & OCR QUALITY

#### 1a. Multi-Column Table OCR Reading Order
PaddleOCR returns text regions in detection order, not left-to-right table order. A 12-column
tooling table will frequently come back scrambled — column 7 before column 3 — because each cell
is treated as an independent text block.

**Solution (already mandated in SCANNED PDF EXTRACTION section — verify compliance)**:
- After calling `PaddleOcrAll.Run(bitmap)`, ALWAYS sort results by bounding box X coordinate
  within each Y-grouped row bucket.
- Y grouping bucket size: `(pageHeightPixels / estimatedRowCount)` — use 30px default.
- Within each bucket, sort ascending by `Region.Center.X`.
- This sort must occur in `ScannedPdfExtractor`, NOT deferred to parsers.
- Write a unit test: `ScannedExtractor_ColumnsReturnedInDetectionOrder_SortedCorrectly`.

#### 1b. Rotated or Landscape PDFs
Some tooling sheets are printed landscape (A3 rotated to A4). PaddleOCR reads rotated text
sideways and produces garbage output.

**Solution (already mandated in PAGE ORIENTATION DETECTION section — verify compliance)**:
- After rendering each page at 300 DPI, run the center-crop orientation check.
- Try 0°, 90°, 180°, 270° rotations and select the one yielding the highest OCR word count.
- Apply the winning rotation to the full-page bitmap BEFORE calling full OCR.
- Record the rotation angle in `ToolingRecord.PageOrientation`.
- Log: `"Page {n} of {file}: rotated {angle}° for orientation correction."`

#### 1c. Low-Scan-Quality PDFs
Fax-quality scans (150 DPI source), skewed pages, coffee stains, ink bleed. Rendering these
at 300 DPI merely enlarges a bad original. Without pre-processing, OCR confidence on bad scans
will land in the 0.4–0.6 range — below the threshold — causing entire pages to be silently dropped.

**Solution (already mandated in IMAGE PRE-PROCESSING PIPELINE section — verify compliance)**:
- `ImagePreprocessor.Preprocess()` must implement all three stages in order:
  1. Grayscale conversion (SkiaSharp pixel-level)
  2. Otsu binarization — compute histogram, find Otsu threshold, binarize each pixel
  3. Deskew — horizontal projection profile, rotate up to ±`MaxDeskewAngleDegrees`
- Apply ONLY when mean pixel brightness < `ImagePreprocessing:BrightnessThreshold` (0.85).
- Dispose source bitmap and preprocessed bitmap in `finally` blocks — no exceptions.
- Write a unit test: `ImagePreprocessor_LowBrightnessInput_PreprocessingApplied`.

#### 1d. Foxit Image Annotation OCR
Some users paste a PNG stamp or image annotation (instead of typed text) over original content.
The amendment detector correctly identifies the annotation and routes to OCR — but the OCR must
read the annotation image content, not just the surrounding text.

**Solution**:
- In `ScannedPdfExtractor`, before calling `PaddleOcrAll.Run(bitmap)`, verify that
  `Sdcb.PdfiumRenderer` is configured to **flatten all annotation layers** into the rendered
  bitmap. Pass `renderFlags` that include annotation rendering.
- Add an integration test or documentation note: `"PdfiumRenderer must flatten annotations —
  verify render flags include FPDFAnnot in Sdcb.PdfiumRenderer API"`.
- If the PdfiumRenderer API does not expose annotation flattening, log a one-time startup
  warning: `"WARNING: Annotation layer flattening not confirmed — image stamp annotations may
  not be OCR-readable. Verify Sdcb.PdfiumRenderer render flags."`.

