### ISSUE 2 — PARSER FRAGILITY

#### 2a. OCR Character Substitution in Critical Fields
PaddleOCR commonly confuses: `0 ↔ O`, `1 ↔ I ↔ l`, `8 ↔ B`, `, ↔ .` (decimal separator).
In a tooling database this is catastrophic — `8.0mm` becomes `B.0mm`, or `T01` becomes `T0I`.

**Solution (already mandated in OCR TEXT CORRECTOR section — verify full implementation)**:
`OcrTextCorrector.CorrectRecord()` must implement all of the following:
- `CorrectToolNumber`: replace `O→0`, `I→1`, `l→1`, `B→8` in the numeric portion after `T`.
- `CorrectNumericField`: replace `O→0`, `I→1`, `l→1`, `B→8`, `S→5`, `Z→2`; normalise `,` to
  `.` as decimal separator; strip non-numeric chars except `.` and `-`.
- `CorrectPartNumber`: context-aware `O/0` resolution — digit-surrounded → `0`, letter-surrounded → `O`.
- `CorrectOperationCode`: `"OP1O"` → `"OP10"`, `"OPI0"` → `"OP10"`.
- `CorrectRevisionCode`: digits after `"REV"` corrected for `I/1`, `O/0`.
Apply to ALL records where `PdfType != PdfType.Digital`.

#### 2b. Merged Cells in Scanned Tables
When a tool has a long description, the CAM programmer may have merged cells across two rows in
the original Excel/Word before printing. OCR sees one wide text block spanning two row heights.
The default ±10px Y-tolerance splits it incorrectly.

**Solution**:
- In all parsers, after splitting tab-separated data lines: if a row has **fewer than 3 tokens**
  AND the next row also has **fewer than 3 tokens**, merge them by joining with a space and
  re-splitting. Attempt this merge once only per row pair.
- If the merged row still has fewer than 3 tokens, skip with a log entry:
  `"SKIP: row '{raw}' — too few tokens after merge attempt (file: {file})"`
- This is already partially specified in ParserA step 4. Verify it is implemented in ParserB
  and ParserC as well.

#### 2c. Header Fields Split Across Two Lines
`Part Description: BLEED VALVE` on line 1 and `BODY` wrapping to line 2. The regex
`[^\n\r]+` captures only the first line. Long part descriptions and machine model names will
be silently truncated.

**Solution (already mandated in PARSERS section — verify regex compliance)**:
- Use the negative-lookahead pattern `(?:(?!\n\s*\w+:).)+` for all multi-value header fields:
  `Tool List`, `Part Description`, `Machine Model`.
- This pattern captures across a newline but stops before the next label (e.g. `Machine:`).
- Verify the regex is `RegexOptions.Singleline | RegexOptions.IgnoreCase` where applicable.
- Write a unit test: `Parse_MultiLinePartDescription_CapturedCorrectly` (already in test list —
  confirm the test uses a two-line description like the sample above).

#### 2d. Decimal Separator Regional Variation
European engineers may use `,` as decimal separator in diameter/length fields (`8,5mm` not
`8.5mm`). Silent data corruption occurs if records are later sorted or queried numerically.

**Solution (part of OcrTextCorrector — verify always applied)**:
- `CorrectNumericField` MUST normalise `,` → `.` as part of its correction pipeline.
- This normalisation applies to ALL records, not just OCR output — apply to Digital records too
  via a separate `NormalizeDecimalSeparators(ToolingRecord record)` call in the parser itself,
  regardless of `PdfType`.
- Add a unit test: `CorrectNumericField_EuropeanDecimalComma_Normalised` with input `"8,5"` →
  expected `"8.5"`, and `"12,50mm"` → `"12.50mm"`.

#### 2e. Empty Tool Rows That Are Continuation Rows
Some templates leave `Tool No.` blank for rows 2–N of a multi-insert tool (e.g. a face mill
with 5 inserts listed separately). The current logic skips rows where `ToolNo` is empty — those
continuation rows are silently lost.

**Solution**:
- In all parsers, replace the blanket "skip if ToolNo empty" rule with:
  ```
  if (ToolNo is empty AND previous row had a valid ToolNo)
      → inherit ToolNo and ToolName from the previous row
      → set Remarks += " [CONTINUATION ROW]"
      → include the record, do NOT skip
  if (ToolNo is empty AND no previous valid row exists)
      → skip (it is a genuine blank row, not a continuation)
  ```
- Track `lastValidToolNo` and `lastValidToolName` as local variables within the row-parsing
  loop in each parser.
- Add unit test: `Parse_ContinuationRowEmptyToolNo_InheritsPreviousToolNo`.

