### ISSUE 3 — FILE SYSTEM & CONCURRENCY

#### 3a. Network Share / UNC Path Latency
Your aerospace company likely stores PDFs on a network drive (`\\SERVER\TOOLS\...`). With 4
parallel workers reading from a UNC path simultaneously, you will hit SMB connection limits
or I/O timeouts.

**Solution**:
- Add a `NetworkPathThrottleParallelism` setting to `ToolingExtractorOptions` (default: `2`).
- In `ExtractionPipelineService`, before creating the parallel worker semaphore:
  ```csharp
  bool isNetworkPath = folderPath.StartsWith(@"\\") ||
      (Path.IsPathRooted(folderPath) &&
       new DriveInfo(Path.GetPathRoot(folderPath)!).DriveType == DriveType.Network);
  int actualParallelism = isNetworkPath
      ? Math.Min(_options.MaxParallelFiles, _options.NetworkPathThrottleParallelism)
      : _options.MaxParallelFiles;
  var semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(actualParallelism);
  ```
- Log at startup if a network path is detected:
  `"Network path detected — throttling parallelism to {actualParallelism} worker(s)."`
- Add `NetworkPathThrottleParallelism: 2` to `appsettings.json` under `ToolingExtractor`.

#### 3b. File Locked by Another Process
PDFs open in Foxit or Adobe while the extractor runs will throw `IOException`. The error
message must explicitly guide the user, not expose a raw CLR exception.

**Solution (already partially in ERROR HANDLING POLICY — verify exact message)**:
- In the per-file `catch (IOException ex)` block in `ExtractionPipelineService`, check:
  ```csharp
  if (ex.Message.Contains("being used by another process",
      StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
  {
      var msg = $"File is locked by another application (Foxit, Adobe, etc.) — " +
                $"close it and re-run the extraction. File: {Path.GetFileName(filePath)}";
      _logger.LogWarning(msg);
      AppendToErrorLog(filePath, "FILE_LOCKED", msg);
  }
  ```
- Do NOT include the full path in user-facing messages (security policy).
- Increment `FailedFiles` and continue.

#### 3c. Filename Encoding Issues
Arabic, Malay, or Chinese characters in filenames (common in Malaysian aerospace environments)
can cause path handling failures on Windows depending on system locale.

**Solution**:
- Always use `Path.GetFullPath(requestedPath)` — already mandated in `ValidateFolderPath`.
- When enumerating files in `FolderScanService`, use:
  ```csharp
  Directory.GetFiles(canonical, "*.pdf", SearchOption.AllDirectories)
  ```
  This uses the .NET runtime's UTF-16 path handling, which is locale-independent on Windows.
- Never use `string.Format` or concatenation to build paths — always use `Path.Combine`.
- Store `SourceFile` as the full relative path using `Path.GetRelativePath(rootFolder, filePath)`,
  which handles Unicode path segments correctly.
- Add a note in README: `"PDF filenames with Unicode characters (Arabic, Chinese, Malay) are
  supported. Ensure .NET 10 runtime is installed; no system locale changes required."`

#### 3d. Duplicate Filenames Across Subdirectories
Two files named `AA000-279_OP10.pdf` in different subfolders produce records with identical
`SourceFile` basenames, making them indistinguishable in the database.

**Solution (already mandated in ToolingRecord model — verify implementation)**:
- `ToolingRecord.SourceFile` MUST store the **full relative path** from the root extraction
  folder, not just the filename. Use:
  ```csharp
  record.SourceFile = Path.GetRelativePath(rootFolderPath, filePath);
  ```
- This means `SourceFile` will be e.g. `"SubfolderA\AA000-279_OP10.pdf"` not just
  `"AA000-279_OP10.pdf"`.
- Verify all UI display, CSV export, and Excel export columns show the relative path.
- Add unit test: `FolderScan_DuplicateFilenames_StoredWithRelativePaths`.

